\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Birefringence validation (dual-model: sectional and phenomenological). A: Sectional model --- mean output $|E_x|^2$ vs fibre length (0--1.5~km), showing the multi-section random walk that gradually scrambles the polarisation from $|E_x|^2 = 1$ toward $0.5$. B: Phenomenological model --- mean output $|E_x|^2$ vs fibre length (0--200~km), showing the gradual $\sqrt  {L}$-dependent rotation that produces a distance-dependent QBER for long-haul BB84. C: Phenomenological model --- mean output $|E_x|^2$ vs temperature at 50~km; the curve is inverted (peak at $\sim $40$^\circ $C) because $|E_x|^2$ is plotted rather than the rotation angle $\theta $ itself, which would show a V-shaped minimum at the null. D: Phenomenological model --- mean output $|E_x|^2$ vs bend radius at 50~km (Ulrich~\cite  {ulrich}). E: Beat length $L_B = \lambda /\Delta n_0$ vs wavelength (Agrawal Eq.~4.1.2) for the sectional model's $\Delta n_0 = 5\times 10^{-8}$. F: Deterministic $\Delta n$ components showing temperature dependence and bend contributions.}}{16}{figure.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:biref}{{6}{16}{Birefringence validation (dual-model: sectional and phenomenological). A: Sectional model --- mean output $|E_x|^2$ vs fibre length (0--1.5~km), showing the multi-section random walk that gradually scrambles the polarisation from $|E_x|^2 = 1$ toward $0.5$. B: Phenomenological model --- mean output $|E_x|^2$ vs fibre length (0--200~km), showing the gradual $\sqrt {L}$-dependent rotation that produces a distance-dependent QBER for long-haul BB84. C: Phenomenological model --- mean output $|E_x|^2$ vs temperature at 50~km; the curve is inverted (peak at $\sim $40$^\circ $C) because $|E_x|^2$ is plotted rather than the rotation angle $\theta $ itself, which would show a V-shaped minimum at the null. D: Phenomenological model --- mean output $|E_x|^2$ vs bend radius at 50~km (Ulrich~\cite {ulrich}). E: Beat length $L_B = \lambda /\Delta n_0$ vs wavelength (Agrawal Eq.~4.1.2) for the sectional model's $\Delta n_0 = 5\times 10^{-8}$. F: Deterministic $\Delta n$ components showing temperature dependence and bend contributions}{figure.caption.12}{}}
